WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.127
- How would you like to
make a partner right now?

00:00:04.127 --> 00:00:06.833
- Oh, oh no, oh no.

00:00:08.043 --> 00:00:10.523
Ew, quid pro quo sexual harassment!

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.570
Oh, is that even consensual?

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.403
(cheerful jingle)

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.390
Hey legal eagles, the James Stone here,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.320
teaching you how to think like a lawyer.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.510
Today we are covering one of the funniest

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.357
legal movies of all time, Liar Liar.

00:00:29.357 --> 00:00:32.300
Now this is the first
in a two-part series.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.360
As always, remember to like and subscribe,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.920
and be sure to comment in
the form of an objection.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.390
I'll either sustain or
overrule your objections,

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.990
and while you're there,
let me know what movie

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.630
or TV show I should do next,

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:46.700
and stick around until
the end of the video

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.390
where I give the first half of Liar Liar

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.720
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.410
So without further ado, let's dig in

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.930
to the first half of Liar Liar.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:57.173
- My dad,

00:00:58.643 --> 00:00:59.680
he's

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.280
a liar.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.990
- A liar?

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:07.260
Oh, I, I'm sure you don't mean a liar.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.670
- Well, he wears a suit and goes to court

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:10.767
and talks to the judge.

00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:13.370
- Oh, oh, I see,

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.507
you mean he's a lawyer.

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:18.170
(relaxed music)

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:21.610
- I object, that is slanderous
and I will take that

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.130
little boy to court and sue
him for all that he is worth.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.810
Uh, you know, or no, it's not defamation,

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:29.643
I'm just kidding.

00:01:29.643 --> 00:01:30.610
- Reede, do you have a moment?

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.590
- I'm sorry, I'm very late,

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:32.940
it's my day to be with my son.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.540
- A couple of reporters
want to talk to you

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.000
about your big win today.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.090
- Oh yeah, how's my hair?

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.120
- Fabulous, you look great.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.780
- So lawyers often have
to talk to the press.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.480
I've talked to journalists
on many occasions

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.800
to talk about recent
developments in the law

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.480
and things that are going on in my cases,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.600
because if you don't do PR
for your client, no one will,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.390
or worse yet, they'll
talk to your opponent

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.770
who will give a completely different spin,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.150
and you've gotta get ahead of that

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.430
to make sure that your side gets out.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.610
- Fred, it's your duty to present

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.041
the strongest case possible.

00:02:04.041 --> 00:02:05.418
- Sort of.
- The strongest case possible

00:02:05.418 --> 00:02:07.369
consistent with the truth.
- Yeah, sort of.

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:08.347
- Will you let the judge
decide what's true?

00:02:08.347 --> 00:02:10.010
That's what he gets paid for.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.243
- Eh.
- You get paid to win.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.070
- So lawyers are bound by the rules

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.180
of professional responsibility and ethics

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.920
for their particular jurisdiction,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.330
and most jurisdictions base their rules

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.030
on the ABA model rules of conduct,

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.120
and under those rules, a
lawyer has an obligation

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.220
of candor to judges and juries.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.770
In other words, they have to be honest

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.560
to those judges that
they're up in front of,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.330
so under the rules of
professional responsibility,

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.350
no single lawyer can lie to a tribunal,

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.830
and in fact, if the lawyer
knows that their client

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.350
is going to lie to a
judge or jury, that lawyer

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:55.400
has an obligation to not
elicit that information.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.010
In fact, the lawyer has
a duty to try to convince

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.940
their client not to offer false testimony

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.230
in front of a tribunal,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.690
so there are plenty of rules
of professional responsibility

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.980
that prevent lawyers from
actively lying to a court.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.810
So under rule 3.3 of the Model
Rules of Professional Conduct

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:16.690
a lawyer can not lie to a judge,

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.670
a lawyer cannot knowingly
elicit false testimony

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.290
from their client, and
in fact, if you know that

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.220
there is contrary legal
authority out there,

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.020
you have to give that
to the judge in addition

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.970
to the legal authority that
actually helps your case.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.180
So this attorney is absolutely right

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.720
that there is a limit to
the kind of representation

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.450
that you can use for a particular client,

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.880
and you can't actively lie
to a judge or jury to help

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.416
your client out, regardless
of what your client wants.

00:03:46.416 --> 00:03:49.230
- Damn it, I completely forgot.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.483
- Oh, what a surprise.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.160
- You are a saint.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.440
I should buy you a gift.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.990
- You did.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.120
- I always do the classy thing.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.510
- I think it's inappropriate for a lawyer

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.700
to rely on his legal
secretary or legal assistant

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.840
to get gifts for his family.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.810
However, I will put in a plug for having

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:11.120
a good legal secretary, they
can really save your life

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.710
when you are working really long hours

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.930
or you have a filing that
is on a tight deadline.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.860
They can really be the
difference between life or death

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.050
in an attorney's office because
they know your schedule,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.500
they know what's going on, they
know what needs to be done,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.300
and often they're knowledgeable enough

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.260
to really help you out when
you're in a tight bind.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.093
- Any calls?

00:04:31.093 --> 00:04:33.252
- Ted Rawling's clerk,
he needs your filing.

00:04:33.252 --> 00:04:34.190
- Tell him it's in the mail.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.910
- Right, you'll do it next week.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.620
Mr. McKinley called.
- Oh man.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:38.740
- To confirm your meeting tomorrow.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.510
- All right, so one of
the things that drives

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.790
all attorneys just completely insane is

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:47.450
the deadlines that they have to adhere to.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.630
When a court or a judge sets a deadline,

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.010
the worst thing you can possibly do

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.150
is let that deadline
lapse because the courts

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:59.890
have a very busy schedule
and you're at their mercy.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:02.950
So if you miss a court-set deadline,

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.060
you can be in a huge amount of trouble.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.120
You could miss one deadline
and that could be grounds

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.550
for getting disbarred or
getting sanctioned by the court,

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.770
and really prejudicing your client.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.490
Often, these deadlines are
what's called jurisdictional,

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.600
which means that if
you miss that deadline,

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.120
your case might get dismissed.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.340
So deadlines are this
malpractice trap for attorneys,

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.820
and I've seen many, many appeals taken

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.490
where the attorney just screwed up,

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.220
they missed a deadline,
they were late in a filing,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.540
and you cannot do what Fletcher
Reede is trying to do here

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.750
and just push off a
deadline when the court

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.760
is calling you up and saying
"you need to file something."

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.930
That could be really
important to the case,

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.982
and you could get horribly sanctioned

00:05:44.982 --> 00:05:47.470
if you fail to adhere to that deadline,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.770
so that's the first time
Fletcher may have been

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.740
disbarred in this particular movie.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.650
- I heard about your
victory, congratulations.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.200
You know, you're making
quite an impression

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.547
on the partnership committee.

00:05:59.547 --> 00:06:03.020
- Oh that's right, you folks
are meeting again soon.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:04.470
I've just been keeping myself so busy

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:07.570
I haven't even thought about it. (laughs)

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:10.410
- Most law firms have
a partnership committee

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.720
that is formed by a
group of equity partners,

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.910
which means that they are actual partners,

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.660
they own equity in the particular law firm

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.340
and they're going to
choose who gets to come in

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.860
as another equity partner
for that law firm.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.970
One of the things that
most people don't know

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.840
about partners in a big law firm

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.930
is that in order to become a partner,

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.470
you have to actually invest, usually,

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:34.820
you have to invest in the firm itself.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.370
So often when you're being upgraded from

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:40.740
an associate to a partner in the law firm,

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.640
you actually have to make
a substantial contribution,

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.780
often on the order of hundreds
of thousands of dollars,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.430
to purchase your equity.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:52.660
So they are voting to let
you in to allow you to pay

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.820
the firm to allow you to be
a partner in that law firm.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.540
Hopefully if you're paying
a substantial amount

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:00.580
to become an equity partner in a law firm,

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.230
it's profitable enough that you will

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.400
recoup your investment and then some.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.260
Another thing that a
lot of people don't know

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.690
about partners in a law
firm is that they often

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.080
don't get paid a salary.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.410
They only get paid a percentage of revenue

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:15.130
or profit in the law firm itself.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:17.860
So often when you go from
an associate in a law firm

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.268
to a partner, you forego a salary

00:07:20.268 --> 00:07:23.430
and you only get paid maybe once a year

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:27.430
based on the general
financials of that entire firm,

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:29.120
so there's a lot of good and a lot of bad

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:30.890
associate with being a partner,

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.810
but obviously Fletcher here
wants to become a partner,

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:36.703
as most people do in a law firm.

00:07:36.703 --> 00:07:38.620
(playful music)

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.540
- Oh, this is good.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.750
This is really smart.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.000
- Thank you.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.430
- Only, well it's not true,

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:50.257
I mean, does that present a problem?

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.400
- So if the lawyer himself can't lie

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.480
to a judge or jury, I really doubt you can

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.880
convince your client to
lie on his or her behalf

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.880
to the judge or jury, so I
think this is a violation

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.830
already of rule 3.3, the duty of candor.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.620
- You're the victim here,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.830
the wife of a cold, distant workaholic,

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:15.742
starved for affection, driven
in the arms of another man.

00:08:15.742 --> 00:08:16.575
- Seven.

00:08:16.575 --> 00:08:17.550
- Yeah whatever.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.180
You're not trying to deny
him what is rightfully his,

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:22.650
all you're insisting on is
what is rightfully yours.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:23.483
- Yeah.

00:08:23.483 --> 00:08:26.610
- And maybe a (scoffs) fraction more.

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:29.380
I think you're bending over backwards.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:33.270
- So ironically, despite
them saying that whatever

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:36.120
was in that legal brief that was a lie,

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.410
the argument that Fletcher
Reede is making here

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:41.830
aren't really lies,
they're just creative ways

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:44.240
of talking about the facts as they exist.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.410
So his rhetorical flourishes
here talking about

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.570
how she's the victim, those can be true

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:55.840
from her point of view, so
it's not necessarily a lie

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:59.030
in and of itself, it's
just a creative argument

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.330
that uses the facts as they exist.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:02.730
I don't know if that would win the day

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.450
against a prenuptial argument
that's pretty cut and dry,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.520
but so far, what he's talking about

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:13.250
probably doesn't breach
rule 3.3 yet, we'll see.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:14.778
- You're right, Mr. Reede,

00:09:14.778 --> 00:09:17.760
I am tired of getting kicked around.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:19.143
- Good for you.

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:25.094
- Thank you, I am so grateful

00:09:25.094 --> 00:09:27.991
to have an attorney I can trust.

00:09:27.991 --> 00:09:29.063
(whimpers)
- Oh god.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.590
All right, so let's talk
about whether it's appropriate

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:38.530
for a lawyer to engage in sexual activity

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:40.560
with his or her client.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.850
Up until last year, California
was one of the few states

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.130
that said that a lawyer could, in fact,

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.390
engage in sexual relations
with his or her client,

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:54.140
so long as that attorney
didn't coerce or force

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.130
that client into that
activity under duress.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:01.250
That rule changed last year
when California Supreme Court

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.830
brought California into
line with most other states

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.854
that says an attorney cannot,
under any circumstances,

00:10:08.854 --> 00:10:11.740
have sexual relations with their client,

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:15.150
unless that relationship existed before

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.270
the attorney client relationship existed.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:19.940
So, at the time that this movie was made,

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:24.520
it was sort of okay for
clients and attorneys

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.930
to have a romantic relationship.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.370
Now, that's not appropriate at all,

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.690
so that's something that
Fletcher really needs

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:35.633
to watch out for, he needs to
be careful with that client.

00:10:37.549 --> 00:10:40.049
(heels click)

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.429
- Oh you are good.

00:10:43.429 --> 00:10:46.603
(chuckles) You are very, very good.

00:10:48.299 --> 00:10:50.900
You know, the Cole case
is worth a truckload

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:52.300
of money to this firm.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:55.773
If you win, I guarantee
you'll make partner.

00:10:55.773 --> 00:10:56.606
- Oh.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:58.833
- In fact,

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.650
how would you like to
make a partner right now?

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:05.690
- Oh, oh no.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:07.560
Oh no.
- I mean, I shouldn't--

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.053
- Ew, quid pro quo sexual harassment.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:13.663
Oh, is that even consensual?

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:16.970
Really, I think that
that could be considered

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:18.840
quid pro quo sexual harassment.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:22.248
She is making it seem like his partnership

00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:27.248
is contingent on some sort of
sexual relationship with her.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:30.868
So this is yet another
example of quid pro quo

00:11:30.868 --> 00:11:33.430
sexual harassment showing up in Hollywood.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:35.660
Mixing romance and an employment

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.060
relationship is always tricky.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:41.670
A jury could look at the
situation and say that

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:45.720
his promotion is premised on
this romantic relationship

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.130
with her and thus it's a quid pro quo.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.833
Always, always tricky, never a good idea.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.380
- You know, the Cole case is
worth a truckload of money

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:58.900
to this firm, if you win, I
guarantee you'll make partner.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.200
- Oh.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:01.900
- I don't think she can
make that guarantee.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.680
A partnership committee is just that,

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:06.360
it's a committee of other partners,

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.280
and if Fletcher doesn't
have the right numbers

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.010
or the right personality or
there are other problems,

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:13.940
then there are all kinds of reasons why

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:15.530
he would not be able to make partner,

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:19.530
even if one particular partner
really goes to bat for him.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:21.540
In fact, while it is
really helpful to have

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.180
at least one partner go
to bat, you need to really

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:26.410
wine and dine the whole
partnership committee

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:28.524
in order to make partner.

00:12:28.524 --> 00:12:33.524
(playful music)
(door bangs)

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:44.750
All right, so fun fact about going to

00:12:44.750 --> 00:12:47.430
counsel table in a courtroom like that,

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.490
normally you wouldn't
be able to walk right up

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.370
to counsel table because
there are a lot of different

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:55.330
cases that are gonna be called
before the judge that day,

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.220
and you may not be the first one,

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:59.170
but if you ever go to a courthouse,

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:02.624
if you look outside the
door to the courtroom,

00:13:02.624 --> 00:13:04.800
then you'll see what's called the docket.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:08.090
The docket will explain what cases

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.260
are up for a hearing that day

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.320
and there will probably be
a dozen of them at least,

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:16.450
and you'll find which number
you are on the docket,

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.418
and odds are they'll go sequentially down

00:13:19.418 --> 00:13:23.090
and leave the most
contentious for the last one

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:24.830
so that they get the most time.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:29.150
So if Fletcher has the first
number on the docket that day,

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:31.740
often you will go and you'll just sit down

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.020
straight at a counsel table,
but before you do that,

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.600
you have to check in with the court clerk,

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:40.090
and in California generally you give them

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:43.920
two copies of a business card
so that they can attach that

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:48.317
to the sheet that talks about
what your case is there for.

00:13:48.317 --> 00:13:50.540
Once you check in with the court clerk

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.640
then you can sit down if
you are the first number

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.003
to be called on the docket.

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.700
- Fletcher.
(chairs squeak)

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:58.960
- Dana.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:00.300
- All right Samantha,

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:02.050
how much will it take to
put an end to all this?

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:03.466
- 50% of your estate.

00:14:03.466 --> 00:14:06.240
- (scoffs) 50%?

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:08.640
With a prenup and proof of adultery?

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:09.700
What's your case?

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.423
- Our case is simply this.

00:14:13.345 --> 00:14:18.262
(tense music)
(stuttering)

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:23.610
- It is pretty normal for
attorneys in the same field

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:27.440
to have a prior
relationship, to know people

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:30.260
by their first name and to talk with them.

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:33.150
What's odd here though is
that Fletcher started out

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:35.980
this movie representing
a criminal defendant,

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:38.060
or presumably someone who
was a criminal defendant,

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.941
and has now jumped into divorce law,

00:14:40.941 --> 00:14:43.570
which you don't really see very often.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.870
So it would also be weird for him to have

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:49.180
this prior relationship with
another divorce attorney

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:52.467
when it's not clear that
he's ever represented

00:14:52.467 --> 00:14:55.530
someone in a marital dispute like this.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:59.835
- All rise for the honorable
Judge Marshall Stevens.

00:14:59.835 --> 00:15:02.653
- Honorable. (scoffs)

00:15:02.653 --> 00:15:06.060
- Good morning, call case number BD09395,

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:11.480
Samantha Cole versus Richard Cole.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:13.300
How we doing this morning, counsel?

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:14.710
- Fine, thank you.

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:16.230
- And you, Mr. Reede?

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.490
- I'm a little upset
about a bad sexual episode

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:22.060
I had last night.
(laughing)

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.120
- So far, this is exactly what happens

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:26.490
when the judge comes into the courtroom

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.780
and calls the hearing to order.

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:31.990
They'll read the case
name and the case number

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:33.860
so that the stenographer
can take that down

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:35.230
and make it part of the official record

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:37.850
so that they have a
record of what's going on,

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:40.330
and he'll make a little bit of small talk

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:44.142
with opposing attorneys before
getting the ball started

00:15:44.142 --> 00:15:46.830
and proceeding into the hearing.

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:49.790
- First, Mr. Reede, I
see where your client--

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.000
- Your Honor, I would like a continuance.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.220
- This case has already been delayed

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:55.870
several times, Mr. Reede.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:57.080
- I realize that, Your Honor,

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:00.063
but I would really, really,
really like a continuance.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.539
- I'll have to hear good cause, counsel.

00:16:04.539 --> 00:16:06.563
- I can't lie.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:09.700
- Commendable, Mr. Reede,
but I'm still waiting

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:12.450
to hear good cause, now
do you have one or not?

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:13.530
- Not.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:15.510
- Motion for continuance denied.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:16.470
(gavel bangs)

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:18.843
Is there any chance of a
settlement in this case?

00:16:18.843 --> 00:16:21.460
- Yeah, so that is right on.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:24.160
Attorneys can ask for
continuances all the time,

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.360
and often, especially in
California state court,

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:30.170
things are going to be delayed
over and over and over again.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:33.360
So if you get to the point
where the case is ready

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.390
to go to trial, then the judge is going to

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:39.330
want it to go forward with due speed.

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:43.208
So you can get a continuance,
but as the judge has

00:16:43.208 --> 00:16:46.700
required here, you're gonna
have to show good cause.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:49.010
Now I think Fletcher
should have been able to

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:51.630
come up with good cause
without having to lie.

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:54.850
He could have said that
he was just brought on

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:57.090
to this case a few days ago,

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:59.680
that is generally considered good cause,

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:03.490
especially if the court
approved the transfer of counsel

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:06.990
because her old lawyer
withdrew from the case

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:10.020
so that Fletcher could
come on and represent her.

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:13.130
So if the court presumably
approved that withdrawal

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:17.392
and substitution, then he
has good cause baked in

00:17:17.392 --> 00:17:21.520
to be able to delay this
case a little bit more.

00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.360
- Mr. Reede has made it abundantly clear

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:25.259
that he has no desire--
- Settle!

00:17:25.259 --> 00:17:27.080
Settle, settle, settle, settle!

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:28.554
- I don't want to settle.

00:17:28.554 --> 00:17:29.387
- Just settle.

00:17:29.387 --> 00:17:32.120
- Mr. Reede, you convinced me yesterday,

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:35.180
I'm the victim here, I'm
starved for affection,

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:37.440
I'm driven into the arms of another man.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:38.273
- Seven.

00:17:38.273 --> 00:17:39.560
- Yeah whatever.

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:42.700
With the story you came up
with, I don't think we can lose.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:44.830
I want to proceed.

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.060
- Mrs. Cole, you don't understand.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:49.710
- Mr. Reede, do we have a settlement?

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:50.543
- No!

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.310
- All right, that's good.

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.220
An attorney has an obligation to provide

00:17:56.220 --> 00:18:00.180
all offers of settlement
to his or her client,

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:04.370
and in the first instance,
Fletcher Reede tried to

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:06.850
settle the case without
talking to his client,

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:09.920
which would be a violation
of the model rules

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.170
of professional conduct,
which would be cause

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:14.830
for disbarment if he
want forward with that,

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:16.740
so watch out for being disbarred,

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:19.410
but ultimately he
conferred with his client,

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.400
provided the terms of the
settlement, and she rejected it.

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:25.500
She has the ability, as
the client she's the one

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.770
that's in charge of the litigation

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:30.010
and she can choose to
reject that settlement

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:31.850
and more forward, that's up to her.

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:33.860
- You know what I pulled you over?

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:37.330
- Depends on how long you
were following me. (cringes)

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:38.912
- Why don't we just take it from the top.

00:18:38.912 --> 00:18:40.050
- Don't talk to the cops.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:41.909
- Here goes, I sped, I
followed too closely--

00:18:41.909 --> 00:18:43.310
- Stop, stop talking to the cop.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:45.640
- I sped some more, I failed
to yield at a crosswalk,

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:46.970
I changed lanes in the intersection,

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:48.310
I changed lanes without signaling

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:50.260
while running a red light and speeding.

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:52.703
- Is that all?

00:18:52.703 --> 00:18:53.536
- No.

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:58.120
I have unpaid parking tickets.

00:18:59.831 --> 00:19:02.360
(groans)
(tender music)

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:03.193
Be gentle.

00:19:04.033 --> 00:19:07.010
- All right, let's talk
about what Fletcher Reede

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:09.160
should have done in this situation,

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:11.600
with the understanding that he can't lie.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:13.330
Just because you can't lie

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:15.990
doesn't mean you have to
tell the police everything,

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.930
so what I would have
recommended, and what Fletcher

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:21.550
should have known if
he was a better lawyer

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:24.230
is that you don't have
to talk to the police.

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:26.480
You almost always have the
right to remain silent.

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:29.550
That's in fact what the
Miranda warning is all about,

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:32.060
you have the right to remain silent.

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:36.100
Now the police can ask
for your identification.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:39.740
They can ask for your driver's
license and your registration

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:42.580
without any real objection,
but that doesn't mean

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.480
that you have to talk to the police,

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.930
nor do you have to answer their questions.

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:49.470
So generally speaking,
and there are of course

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:53.520
exceptions to this, but
Fletcher didn't need

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:56.420
to tell the police
everything that he had done.

00:19:56.420 --> 00:19:58.590
He could have just sat there silently

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.140
and let the police draw
their own conclusions.

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:03.780
Now many police officers will tell you

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:06.990
that if you remain silent during a stop,

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:10.310
that may give them probable
cause or reasonable suspicion

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.740
that something else is
wrong and that they can do

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:15.280
a more thorough search, but here,

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.570
Fletcher doesn't appear
to have any evidence

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:20.880
of other wrongdoing except
for those old parking tickets.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:24.080
It's not like he had a bunch
of cocaine in the trunk

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.460
of his car and he didn't want
the police to search his car.

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:30.280
So potentially, the police
could have responded

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:33.260
to his silence with the assumption of

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:34.970
reasonable suspicion of some other crime,

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:36.500
but they wouldn't have found anything else

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:38.960
and they would have only
be able to write him

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:41.950
one or maybe two ticket infractions.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:45.140
So if Fletcher had been a better attorney,

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.238
he just would have stayed silent,

00:20:47.238 --> 00:20:50.058
given the police his ID and registration,

00:20:50.058 --> 00:20:53.470
and taken whatever ticket
the police gave him

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:57.153
instead of making his
situation so, so much worse.

00:20:58.160 --> 00:20:59.453
- You scratched my car!

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:01.493
- Where?

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:04.303
- Right there.

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:06.943
- Oh, there.

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.161
That was already there.

00:21:10.161 --> 00:21:11.613
- Why you,

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:14.343
you liar!

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.307
You know what I'm gonna do about this?

00:21:17.307 --> 00:21:18.140
- What?

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:20.150
- Nothing, because if I take
you to small claims court,

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:21.580
it'll just drain eight
hours out of my life

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:22.800
and you probably won't show up,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.070
and if I finally got the judgment,

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:26.880
you'd just stiff me anyway,
so what I'm gonna do

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:28.940
is piss and moan like an impotent jerk

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:32.563
and then bend over and
take it up the tailpipe.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:35.970
- You been here before haven't you?

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:39.020
- So Fletcher points out
that while would be correct

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:41.910
in suing this tow yard for
the damage that they have done

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:45.183
to his car, it would
be very time intensive

00:21:45.183 --> 00:21:49.130
and even if he won, he
might never see the money

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:51.020
to compensate him for that damage.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:52.630
That's called being judgment proof,

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:56.220
where even if someone gets a
valid judgment against you,

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:58.650
you have so few funds
that you couldn't even pay

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:01.380
that judgment even if
you got a court order.

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.250
So that happens all that time,
where sometimes the people

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:08.490
that commit civil wrongs
or do illegal acts

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:11.580
are the ones that are least
able to pay for the damages

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.880
that they cause, so Fletcher finds himself

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.610
on the other end of the spectrum
when it comes to law suits,

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:21.950
where he has a valid law suit presumably,

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:24.420
but he'll never see
money as a result of it,

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:26.030
which is, it's an issue.

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:28.540
- Hello Judge Stevens, hi, Fletcher Reede,

00:22:28.540 --> 00:22:30.930
I'm scheduled to be in
your court in half an hour.

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:32.100
- Oh, talking to the judge.

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:33.940
- I badly, badly need a continuance.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:35.957
Ill, am I ill?

00:22:35.957 --> 00:22:38.570
That is the perfect
question for you to ask.

00:22:38.570 --> 00:22:40.750
Greta please lie to him for me.

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:43.750
- Okay, I have never heard of any instance

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.870
where a particular lawyer
was able to call up

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:49.290
the judge's chambers and get the judge,

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:51.370
the actual judge on the phone.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.200
That basically should never happen.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:56.760
There are times when
you can call up chambers

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.510
and eventually get the judge on the phone,

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:01.030
but in almost all of those situations,

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:03.880
you need opposing counsel present as well.

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:07.420
For example, if you had a deposition where

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:10.600
you thought the witness
was behaving improperly

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:13.500
such that you had to
shut the deposition down

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.090
and the witness would have to be recalled,

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:18.820
well you could threaten and
follow through with your threat

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:21.890
to call the judge and
report that behavior.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:23.490
You'd put the judge on speaker phone

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.440
and talk about the
situation that you're in

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:27.970
so that you can hash it
out right there and then,

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:30.180
so you don't have to call the witness back

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:33.810
to do another deposition
and incur additional costs,

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:36.590
but that's a situation where
even with the judge present,

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:39.120
you're going to have the other side there

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.510
so that you have a communication between

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:45.260
all of the parties, not just
one party and the judge.

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:47.440
These are called ex parte communications,

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.840
the communications that
take place outside of court,

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:54.730
and I've never, ever heard of a time when

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:58.190
an attorney could just pick up
the phone and call the judge.

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:00.710
That kind of ex parte
communication would be

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:03.721
very, very frowned upon and would be

00:24:03.721 --> 00:24:06.730
potentially grounds for sanctions because

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:08.990
the other side doesn't
know what's going on.

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:11.930
So that really should never happen.

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:15.110
- Mr. Reede, several
years ago a friend of mine

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:16.945
had a burglar on her roof, a burglar.

00:24:16.945 --> 00:24:17.778
- Oh no.

00:24:17.778 --> 00:24:18.790
- He fell through the kitchen sky light,

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:21.040
landed on a cutting board
on a butcher's knife,

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:22.050
cutting his leg.

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:25.350
The burglar sued my
friend, he sued my friend

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:27.860
and because of guys like you, he won.

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:32.310
My friend had to pay the
burglar $6,000, is that justice?

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:33.143
- No.

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:37.032
I'd have got him 10.
(laughs)

00:24:37.032 --> 00:24:39.570
- All right, here's the
thing about that example

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.900
about the burglar
falling through the roof.

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:45.330
That is a complete urban myth.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:48.980
I hate hearing that example,
it never, ever existed.

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:52.720
That fictitious example
was created specifically

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.360
by politicians in order to argue for

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:56.920
something called tort reform.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:59.210
In other words, making
it harder for plaintiffs

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:02.040
to be able to sue individuals and entities

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.980
for harms that they have befallen them.

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:07.990
Now you can agree with tort
reform or you can disagree

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:11.200
with tort reform, but
the particular example

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:13.820
of a burglar falling through a sky light

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:16.370
into a kitchen and then
suing the homeowner

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:20.300
simply never happened, it
is just a piece of fiction

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:24.560
that was created for argument
rather than legal precedent.

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:27.650
So if you hear someone use that example,

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:29.983
ask to see where that case came from

00:25:29.983 --> 00:25:33.400
because I guarantee that is an urban myth

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:36.050
and not a piece of judicial precedent.

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:39.290
And on top of that, if that
example ever did happen

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:41.710
in the real world, there
are all kinds of reasons

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:44.100
why the homeowner would succeed.

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:46.380
I don't wanna get into the
details of the difference

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.580
between an invitee and a trespasser,

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:52.320
and the duty that one property
owner owes to another,

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:55.580
but suffice to say there
are a lot of reasons why

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:58.610
a homeowner would be
successful in defending a suit

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:01.520
against a burglar who had
fallen through a sky light.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:04.360
So just know that that particular example

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:07.990
is completely fictitious
and make up for the point of

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:12.320
political argument and has no
real basis in the law itself.

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.220
All right, that was the
first half of Liar Liar.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:17.040
Next week we'll get to the trial,

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:19.200
including the question
of whether the prenup

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:22.740
and the marriage itself
was void due to fraud,

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:24.690
but now it's time to give the first half

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:26.465
a grade for legal realism.

00:26:26.465 --> 00:26:28.116
(gavel bangs)

00:26:28.116 --> 00:26:29.610
There is a lot going for Liar Liar.

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:32.930
You've got the real books
that people use in practice.

00:26:32.930 --> 00:26:35.670
It was filmed apparently
at a real law office,

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:37.020
because it was right across the street

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:38.830
from my old law office,

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:41.160
and you've got some of the
dialogue that's pretty realistic.

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:43.808
On the other hand, you've
got completely insane

00:26:43.808 --> 00:26:47.130
things happening due to
a curse that required him

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:50.700
to say only the truth,
you've got a lawyer who

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:52.640
doesn't know when to shut up.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:55.940
All in all, I'm gonna give Liar Liar a B+.

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:58.140
Not a bad effort but there's definitely

00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:00.830
room for improvement,
and we'll see how you do

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:02.800
next time in the second half.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:04.880
All right, do you agree with that B+?

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:06.880
Leave your objections in the comments

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:09.750
and let me know what you
think I should review next.

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:11.370
In the meantime, check out this playlist

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:14.010
I put together that includes
all of my prior reactions,

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:17.020
including Suits, Better
Call Saul, and tons more.

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:20.163
So click on this playlist
and I'll see you in court.

